#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199450 :tutorial/content "Consider a transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}].$$\n\nThe points in the $(u,v)$ plane where $\\mathbf T$ is *locally* non-invertible are known as **critical points**.\n\nWe've already seen that $\\mathbf T$ is locally non-invertible when the Jacobian determinant is zero. Therefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf T$ are the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)}=0.$$\n\nLet's compute the critical points of the following transformation:\n\n$$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}2{u}^{2}-3{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =4u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-6v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =|\\begin{aligned}4u & -6v \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ & =4u\\cdot 1-(-6v)\\cdot (-1) \\\\ & =4u-6v.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe function has critical points when the Jacobian equals zero. So, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =0 \\\\ 4u-6v & =0 \\\\ 2u-3v & =0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the critical points of $\\mathbf{T}$ consist of those points that lie on the line $2u-3v=0$ in the $uv$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c544e3d3ecbdeaf7dc372b40ee0f37cb14e3ba292b8180a263c91103a9efe61.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5f47e688-774b-5c89-a90d-ead52a871598" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4312 :tutorial/title "Critical Points of Transformations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199460 :tutorial/content "Let's now state the so-called **inverse function theorem** for single-variable functions:\n\n*If $f: U \\to \\Bbb R$ is continuously differentiable on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ $f(a) = b$ and $f'(a)\\neq 0$ for $a\\in U,$ then*\n\n- *$f$ is invertible in some neighborhood of $a,$*\n- *$f^{-1},$ the inverse of $f,$ is continuously differentiable in a neighborhood of $b,$ and*\n- *the derivative of $f^{-1}$ is given by* $$\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(b) = \\dfrac{1}{f'(a)} =\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(b))}\\,.$$\n\nTo explain this concretely, consider the graph below:\n\n![](images/4d/4dfd8b142b594a2ca4f0e39f9b8ca1e04018bf0efd63f9dc2235bd61e9f9d70e.png)\n\nIn this example, the function $f$ is continuously differentiable at $x=a$ and has a nonzero derivative. As a result, $f^{-1}$ exists in a neighborhood of $x=b$ and is continuously differentiable at this point.\n\nOn the other hand, if $f$ is continuously differentiable at $x=a$ but $f'(a) = 0,$ then one of two things can happen:\n\n- **Case 1**: The function $f$ does not have a local inverse at $a.$\n\n![](images/04/04ee310e6068e517aec42a67fa83837986f8807e1250b89b52e7a62e3975bb38.png)\n\n In the example above, $f$ is not one-to-one in a neighborhood of $a,$ so it's not invertible there.\n- **Case 2**: The function $f$ has a local inverse at $a,$ but its inverse is not continuously differentiable at $b.$\n\n![](images/3e/3e2738a16f999d503fb295f4fd6535a854884468a4d190ffa537a4ec9295490c.png)\n\n In the example above, $f$ is invertible in a neighborhood of $a,$ but $f^{-1}$ is not differentiable at $x=b$ since it has a vertical tangent at this point." :tutorial/id #uuid "c45037f7-31c7-5911-a00d-edaa4c5b86e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7036 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199477 :tutorial/content "The inverse function theorem can be generalized to transformations too. We state it below:\n\n*Let $\\mathbf T:U \\to \\Bbb R^2$ be a $C^1$ (continuously differentiable) transformation on an open set $U\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ where* $$\\mathbf{T}: [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}] \\longrightarrow [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}], \\qquad \\mathbf{T}\\left([\\begin{aligned}{u}_{0} \\\\ {v}_{0}\\end{aligned}] \\right) = [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{aligned}].$$\n\n*Further, suppose that $(u_0,v_0)$ is **not** a critical point of $\\mathbf T.$* Then,\n\n- *$\\mathbf T$ is locally invertible at $(u_0,v_0),$*\n- *$\\mathbf T^{-1},$ the inverse of $\\mathbf T,$ is a $C^1$ function in a neighborhood of $(x_0,y_0),$ and*\n- *the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} ,$ is given by* $$\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v)}{\\partial (x, y)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right)^{-1}.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "04daa2a7-b824-543f-b8d6-79a5b54e4799" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7678 :tutorial/title "The Inverse Function Theorem for Transformations"}]}
